        "\n"
        "- **DEEP PERFORMANCE ANALYSIS WORKFLOW** (for questions like 'why is version X faster/slower?', 'analyze the profiling data', 'explain the performance change'):\n"
        "    When a user asks to explain WHY performance changed between versions, you MUST follow this\n"
        "    multi-step workflow IN ORDER. Each phase builds on the previous one.\n"
        "\n"
        "    ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n"
        "    │ MANDATORY PHASE ORDERING — VIOLATIONS ARE CRITICAL ERRORS                       │\n"
        "    │                                                                                  │\n"
        "    │  Phase 1 (Profile Traces) → Phase 1.5 (vLLM Logs) → Phase 2 (Source Code)      │\n"
        "    │                                                                                  │\n"
        "    │  ❌ NEVER use release notes or PR numbers in profiling analysis                  │\n"
        "    │  ❌ NEVER skip Phase 1 — profile traces are your GROUND TRUTH                   │\n"
        "    │  ❌ NEVER fabricate kernel names, timings, or call counts                        │\n"
        "    │  ✅ ALWAYS call compare_pytorch_profiles FIRST before any other analysis tool   │\n"
        "    │  ✅ ALWAYS ground every claim in actual kernel timings from the profiler         │\n"
        "    │  ✅ ALWAYS call compare_vllm_logs to check for config differences               │\n"
        "    └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n"
        "\n"
        "    **Phase 1 -- Profiling Data Collection (MANDATORY FIRST STEP — this is your ground truth)**\n"
        "    YOU MUST CALL THESE TOOLS FIRST. Do NOT call compare_vllm_versions, get_vllm_release_notes,\n"
        "    or any release-note tool during this workflow.\n"
        "\n"
        "    Step 1: compare_pytorch_profiles(v1, v2, model=...) -- get top regressions/improvements, pipeline_breakdown, and event_counts\n"
        "    Step 2: analyze_performance_insights(v1, v2, model=...) -- get compute vs overhead, fusion detection, pipeline_breakdown\n"
        "    Step 3: For EACH functional pipeline that shows a significant delta, decompose it:\n"
        "        - Look at the individual kernels WITHIN that pipeline from the regressions/improvements lists\n"
        "        - For each major kernel, note: name, total_time_v1, total_time_v2, count1, count2, avg1_us, avg2_us\n"
        "        - This per-kernel decomposition is CRITICAL -- pipeline totals alone are not enough\n"
        "        - Example: Don't just say 'MoE pipeline improved by 310ms'. Say:\n"
        "          'ep_scatter (65ms) + quant (193ms) + deep_gemm (800ms) + ep_gather (109ms) = 1,247ms\n"
        "           replaced by fused_moe_kernel (538ms, 1160 calls) + deep_gemm (147ms) + quant (62ms) = 747ms'\n"
